    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "## Problem\n\nIn Neovim 0.13, multicursor yanks are stored in cursor-local registers while a multicursor session is active.\n\nThis works correctly when yanking and putting within the same session: each cursor puts the text originally yanked by that cursor.\n\nHowever, when the multicursor session ends, the yanked contents are concatenated into a single register. If a new multicursor session is started, pressing p puts the entire concatenated content at every cursor, rather than distributing the original yanked elements.\n\n## Steps to reproduce:\n\n- Create a multicursor session with 3 cursors.\n- Yank three different strings (foo, bar, baz), one per cursor.\n- Exit the multicursor session.\n- Create a new multicursor session with 3 cursors at different locations.\n- Press p.\n\nActual behavior:\n\nEach cursor puts the concatenated content:\n\n```\nfoo\nbar\nbaz\n```\n\n## Expected behavior:\n\nEach cursor puts its corresponding yanked element:\n\n```\nCursor 1 → foo\nCursor 2 → bar\nCursor 3 → baz\n```\n\n## Proposed behavior\n\nPreserve the individual elements of the most recent multicursor yank across multicursor sessions, so that a subsequent multicursor put can distribute them among the new cursors in order.\n\nIf the last multicursor yank produced **N elements**, the put operation should:\n\n- Distribute the N elements across the first N cursors, in order.\n- If there are more than N cursors, put an empty string at each remaining cursor (effectively doing nothing).\n- If there are fewer than N cursors, put only the first elements, leaving the remaining ones unused.\n\nFor example, if the last yank produced `foo`, `bar`, and `baz`:\n\n| New cursor count | Result |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | `foo`, `bar` |\n| 3 | `foo`, `bar`, `baz` |\n| 5 | `foo`, `bar`, `baz`, `\"\"`, `\"\"` |\n\n## Alternative 1: require matching cursor counts\n\nIf distributing a partial set of elements is undesirable, an alternative would be to perform no put operation when the number of active cursors differs from the number of elements in the last multicursor yank.\n\nIn this case:\n\n- **M = N:** distribute the yanked elements one per cursor.\n- **M ≠ N:** do nothing.\n\n## Alternative 2: introduce a dedicated put command\n\nAnother option would be to introduce a new command, such as `gp`, that performs the distributed put described above, while leaving the existing behavior of `p` unchanged.\n\nThis would preserve backward compatibility and allow users to explicitly choose between:\n\n- **`p`**: put the concatenated register content at every cursor (current behavior).\n- **`gp`**: distribute the individual elements of the last multicursor yank across the active cursors, in order, using the rules described above.\n\nThis approach would make distributed putting an opt-in operation without changing the existing multicursor put semantics.",
