            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "@justinmk thank you\n\n> This is a fair bit of complexity, so it depends on how common the use-case really is.\n\nThe most common use case in my opinion is the following:\n\nGoal: Copy the TEXT_X words and replace the corresponding TEXT_Y words with them.\n\n```python\nabc .... TEXT_X_1 bla bla\nabc .... TEXT_X_2 bla bla\nabc .... TEXT_X_3 bla bla\n\n# [... after a lot of lines, or in another buffer]\n\nif True:\n    some code\n    some code\n    lorem ipsum TEXT_Y_1 test test\nif True:\n    lorem ipsum TEXT_Y_2 test test\nif True:\n    some code\n    lorem ipsum TEXT_Y_3 test test\n```\n\nA very comfortable way to do that with multicursor would be:\n- go to the first \"abc\", add 2 cursors below (or alternatively, search for 'abc' and then `[count]Q`), jump to T with one or more `w`, then `ye` for copy them.\n- exit multicursor, move many lines away or to another buffer.\n- add a cursor at the beginning of each \"lorem\" (with e.g. search for 'lorem' and then `[count]Q` OR by using a plugin as https://github.com/colomb8/mc-cavallo.nvim). Note: this use case makes sense only if the user uses a quick way to add multicursors.\n- jump to each TEXT_Y and replace them with e.g. `vep`.\n\nThis workflow is already feasible with other editors like Sublime Text with selection and C-d for add a cursor to the next occurrence.",
